{"curriculum_hash":"c36abbb213e59791ecc9684122d793c016c2f017ba79172441d89540c2937d52","curriculum_source_hash":"12522f6f8810a36dab3ea60cff105a303c0295fa61695e271732d61f6a365047","gate_source_hash":"96d9c5ac60a3ea06692f2f75f6bd35ec4f8893f4db461386bb43c3e0dcec65f0","recipes":[{"accepted":true,"document":{"arguments":{"answer_contract":{"kind":"exact"},"answer_expr":"p*u+q*v","constraints":[{"left":{"add":[{"mul":[{"lit":2},"p"]},"q"]},"op":"ne","right":{"lit":2}}],"distractors":[],"hints":["Use the product rule, then bring each exponent outside its logarithm."],"params":{"p":{"kind":"choice","values":[-3,-1,"0.5",3]},"q":{"kind":"choice","values":[-3,-2,-1,1]}},"samples":[{"expected":"-3*u+(-3)*v","params":{"p":-3,"q":-3}},{"expected":"-3*u+(-2)*v","params":{"p":-3,"q":-2}},{"expected":"-3*u+(-1)*v","params":{"p":-3,"q":-1}},{"expected":"-3*u+(1)*v","params":{"p":-3,"q":1}},{"expected":"-1*u+(-3)*v","params":{"p":-1,"q":-3}},{"expected":"-1*u+(-2)*v","params":{"p":-1,"q":-2}},{"expected":"-1*u+(-1)*v","params":{"p":-1,"q":-1}},{"expected":"-1*u+(1)*v","params":{"p":-1,"q":1}},{"expected":"0.5*u+(-3)*v","params":{"p":"0.5","q":-3}},{"expected":"0.5*u+(-2)*v","params":{"p":"0.5","q":-2}},{"expected":"0.5*u+(-1)*v","params":{"p":"0.5","q":-1}},{"expected":"3*u+(-3)*v","params":{"p":3,"q":-3}},{"expected":"3*u+(-2)*v","params":{"p":3,"q":-2}},{"expected":"3*u+(-1)*v","params":{"p":3,"q":-1}},{"expected":"3*u+(1)*v","params":{"p":3,"q":1}}],"solution_sketch":"The argument is positive for every chosen exponent. The product and power rules give ${p}\\log_b2+({q})\\log_b3$. Replace the two logarithms by their definitions and simplify the coefficients of $u$ and $v$.","statement":"Let $b>0$ with $b\\ne1$, $u=\\log_b 2$, and $v=\\log_b 3$. Express $\\log_b\\left(2^{{{p}}}\\cdot3^{{{q}}}\\right)$ in terms of $u$ and $v$."},"kind":"template","kp_id":"logarithm-properties/kp3"},"reason":null},{"accepted":true,"document":{"arguments":{"answer_contract":{"kind":"exact"},"answer_expr":"min(a-180*floor(a/180),180-(a-180*floor(a/180)))","constraints":[],"distractors":[],"hints":["First add or subtract complete turns to place the ray in one standard turn."],"params":{"a":{"kind":"choice","values":[-750,-690,-480,-390,-45,375,405,555,510,690,780,1110]}},"samples":[{"expected":"30","params":{"a":-750}},{"expected":"30","params":{"a":-690}},{"expected":"60","params":{"a":-480}},{"expected":"30","params":{"a":-390}},{"expected":"45","params":{"a":-45}},{"expected":"15","params":{"a":375}},{"expected":"45","params":{"a":405}},{"expected":"15","params":{"a":555}},{"expected":"30","params":{"a":510}},{"expected":"30","params":{"a":690}},{"expected":"60","params":{"a":780}},{"expected":"30","params":{"a":1110}}],"solution_sketch":"Add or subtract whole $360^\\circ$ turns to obtain a representative in $[0,360)$. In Quadrant I use that angle; in II measure back to $180^\\circ$, in III measure forward from $180^\\circ$, and in IV measure back from $360^\\circ$. Every selected ray is off the axes, so the resulting gap is acute.","statement":"A signed rotation is ${a}^\\circ$. Reduce full turns and find its reference angle in degrees as a number."},"kind":"template","kp_id":"reference-angles/kp3"},"reason":null},{"accepted":true,"document":{"arguments":{"answer_contract":{"kind":"exact"},"answer_expr":"signcase(p*p+q*q-r*r,[120,90,60])","constraints":[{"left":"p","op":"lt","right":"q"},{"left":{"add":["p","q"]},"op":"gt","right":"r"},{"left":{"add":["p","r"]},"op":"gt","right":"q"},{"left":{"mul":[{"sub":[{"add":[{"mul":["p","p"]},{"mul":["q","q"]}]},{"mul":["r","r"]}]},{"sub":[{"sub":[{"add":[{"mul":["p","p"]},{"mul":["q","q"]}]},{"mul":["r","r"]}]},{"mul":["p","q"]}]},{"add":[{"sub":[{"add":[{"mul":["p","p"]},{"mul":["q","q"]}]},{"mul":["r","r"]}]},{"mul":["p","q"]}]}]},"op":"eq","right":{"lit":0}}],"distractors":[],"hints":["Identify the side opposite the requested angle, then rearrange the cosine rule using the other two sides."],"params":{"p":{"kind":"choice","values":[6,8,9,10,12,14,15]},"q":{"kind":"choice","values":[8,10,12,15,16,20]},"r":{"kind":"choice","values":[10,13,14,15,17,20,21,25,26,28]}},"samples":[{"expected":"90","params":{"p":6,"q":8,"r":10}},{"expected":"120","params":{"p":6,"q":10,"r":14}},{"expected":"60","params":{"p":6,"q":16,"r":14}},{"expected":"60","params":{"p":8,"q":15,"r":13}},{"expected":"90","params":{"p":8,"q":15,"r":17}},{"expected":"90","params":{"p":9,"q":12,"r":15}},{"expected":"120","params":{"p":9,"q":15,"r":21}},{"expected":"60","params":{"p":10,"q":16,"r":14}},{"expected":"90","params":{"p":12,"q":16,"r":20}},{"expected":"120","params":{"p":12,"q":20,"r":28}},{"expected":"120","params":{"p":14,"q":16,"r":26}},{"expected":"90","params":{"p":15,"q":20,"r":25}}],"solution_sketch":"The side opposite the requested angle is ${r}$. Rearranging the cosine rule gives $\\cos A=({p}^2+{q}^2-{r}^2)/(2\\cdot{p}\\cdot{q})$. Simplify this exact ratio and match it to its triangle angle between $0^\\circ$ and $180^\\circ$.","statement":"A triangle has side lengths ${p}$, ${q}$, and ${r}$. Find the angle opposite the side of length ${r}$, in degrees; give a number."},"kind":"template","kp_id":"law-of-cosines/kp3"},"reason":null}],"schema_version":1}
